  "chapter_id": "neuroscience--brain-disorders--ch01-neurodegenerative-diseases",
  "title": "What Is Neurodegeneration?",
  "emoji": "🩺",
  "color": "#5B21B6",
  "description": "A short lesson to help you apply What Is Neurodegeneration?.",
  "is_free": true,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Neurons die every day. In neurodegeneration, they die far too fast and the brain cannot compensate.",
      "visual": "📉"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Neurodegenerative diseases share a common feature: progressive loss of specific neuron populations.",
      "visual": "🔬"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Misfolded proteins accumulate, clump together, and poison the cells they invade—a toxic chain reaction.",
      "visual": "🧬"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Different diseases attack different proteins and regions, which is why symptoms vary so widely.",
      "visual": "🗺️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "By the time symptoms appear, the brain has often already lost 60-80% of the affected neurons.",
      "visual": "⏳"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Neurodegeneration is silent for years. By the time you notice, the damage is deep.",
      "visual": "🕳️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What is a common feature of neurodegenerative diseases?",
    "options": [
      "Misfolded proteins that accumulate and damage neurons",
      "Rapid neuron growth",
      "Excessive myelination"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
